Z||000018||Seanh||04-02-2001||12:15 AM||||Seems to me like it would suck in hot air from the engine compartment, wouldn't it? Could that area be sealed off from the engine compartment, and forced to suck throught the vent in the side. I am not worried about water getting in there, but just wondering why it is a cold air intake, when it looks like it would suck in hotter air then the stock system? Im probally wrong, but I wanna know why.||||reg||1
Z||000019||DaRkLoRD||04-02-2001||12:18 AM||||Pardon my ignorance on this subject.... I understand the concept of exhaust backpressure and it's effects on torque, but I'm not clear on how an open intake can reduce torque. Can anyone explain this?<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000020||fyerstarter||04-02-2001||02:14 AM||||Haha I have never seen such opposition to such a widely known and proven performance modification. The problem with stock cars is there is always the compromise between performance and reliability, comfort, noise, emmisions, whatever. Any car manufacturer could make any car a race car, but they don't becuase there are a lot of people out there who aren't willing to make the sacrifices. That's what modifying cars is all about, you sacrifice one aspect for another. It's funny though how all the "stock people" seem to be so concerned about the practicality or effectiveness of anything that wasn't designed by the "all knowing manufacturer". <P>There is no question whether or not this is a performance modification. The more and colder air you can get into the motor the more efficiently an engine will run. This is the same principle as a turbocharger or a supercharger (which no-one would dispute the performance enhancement of) work on, but to a lesser degree.<P>So what you only get 5 hp increase?, it's better than none and it looks and sounds 110% better than stock. <P><BR>Sleeper or Eric:  Is the intake anchored down in any way? Do you have a problem with the pipe banging around on acceleration/deceleration?  I did with mine so I had to fab up a bracket to hold it in place(it's really ugly tho  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I need to redo it soon)||||reg||1
Z||000021||WKDFIRO||04-02-2001||03:45 AM||||I would have to disagree with Standard's position on the proper calibration of the ass-o-meter. <P>Of course it all depends on how truly "in tune" you are with your car.  I would not try to tell someone that they are wrong when they say they could feel an improvement in acceleration. How do I know that the person with the "seat of the pants" feeling isn't a regular at the strip, or the parking lot tracks (SCCA) or just spends enough time in his car to know that it is doing something it didn't before? <P>Yes I agree whole-heartedly that the dyno and the time slips seperate the hi-po stickers from the true horsepower gains. But I wouldn't rule out the ass-o-meter completely. I would rather hear about POSSIBLE improvements on performance based on initial reactions and then make my own decisions rather than tell someone what they are full of right off the bat.<P>I agree with Taijiguy. The stock intake is well designed. For what its designed for. It is a true-cold air intake, I agree. But then its also designed to not make noise. Its also designed for enviroments with snowbanks, dusty roads, and most importantly of all - its designed to save money on manufacturing costs by being cheap to mass produce and/or utilizing existing parts off the shelf. <P>Besides I don't imagine that the Camaros and Firebirds with RAM AIR mind at all about their filters getting dirty a LOT. It goes with the territory.<P>BTW kevin- its way easier to make an Iron Duke true Ram Air since all you really do is take out the air box and replace it with straight pipe. I used the Holly style intake scoop and had time slips showing 2/10s quicker times and between 2 and 3 miles faster than stock (I did the intake after I had put on the Hooker tri-Y header and it gave me an extra mile in speed.) with a K&N filter replacing the stock filter. <P>And I felt that improvement before I went to the track! HA! ||||reg||12
Z||000022||c m west||04-02-2001||01:12 PM||||Ok lets see here, we take a 5/8" i.d. K&N breather and put it on the breather tube going to the valve cover. We sell them with or without K&N filters. Yes - the tube IS anchored down securely in place and will NEVER give you rattling problems if installed correctly <P>To date we havent ripped anybody of, and I have no intention to, this started out as a hobby, and in most cases still is - when you specialize in only one type of car - YOU CANNOT AFFORD TO **** YOUR CUSTOMERS - and if I have made any mistakes with anything, I always tell the person to give me a call and we will take care of it - IMMEDIATLY, like dad always said, " Your only as reputable as your last screw-up" - so we like to keep the screw ups to a minimum, actually closer to none at all - remember, we are only human, working on nearly 20 year old cars that the general public disrespects, trying to give it the attention it always deserved.<P> For anyone interested in out products give us a call and talk our ear off, thats why I always give out the number - no worries.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000023||Formula88||04-02-2001||01:46 PM||||Eric, I like what I'm hearing about West Coast Fiero, but gotta question.  With the K&N breather instead of the PCV pipe, have you ever noticed any problem with crankcase ventilation?  I've done this on old cars before when putting on open element air cleaners, but wasn't sure how picky a "modern" computer controlled car would be.  Have you guys dyno'ed your air intake?  I'm not so much interested in the performance increase, cuz it's likely to be only 1 or 2 HP, but the SOUND!  I rigged up a K&N cone on my intake and it sounded SO good.  Now I have to get an intake that sounds thta good without looking jerry-rigged.  I like the fact that you seem to put more engineering into your design than the typical back yard mechanic trying to make a buck.  The cost may be a bit more in some cases, but I have noticed a distinct lack of compaints from your customers here.  I'd think this forum would put you out of business real quick if your work was shoddy, so you must be doing something right...||||reg||10
Z||000024||c m west||04-02-2001||02:05 PM||||well thank you, just want to let the people here know that we ARE NOT here to bend you over, just here to make your car perform to the customers expectations.<P> You have to remember that the computer is only there to put gas in the engine, thats it. With the tube not connected to the intake this means there is no engine vacum pulling out pressure from the crank case, instead, it comes out to the atmosphere on its own free will, there by eliminating one more power robbing feature ( as miniscule as it might be ) that the stock system has to do.<P>I'm not here to force anybody into buying what we have, just here to help keep these cars on the road, with the occasional hot rodding to destroy the imports.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||1FST2M6||04-02-2001||03:24 PM||||have you guys thought about making a plug in resistor to ditch the MAT sensor? i've done this to lie to the computer to make it think its a 40*ish intake charge all the time. I saw the your mat sensor isn't in the air stream just on the tube. the extra welding and fitting might me more expensive than a resistor and female plug. Does anyone have Dyno numbers or track numbers for this yet? i picked up 2 tenths in the 1/8th ditching the stock stuff and adding a case  breather. The HKS filter is washable and easy to replace if somthing ever happens to it.. and uum .. he'd have to drive through about 14" of water to suck up any water... and his car wouldn't make it anyway... nor would his seats.. the radio...<P>uum sleeper.. you might want to wrap the intake tube with header tape or heat reflective tape from Thermo-Tec. Mine is close to the exhaust like yours is and it got REALLY warm.. i wrapped it.. and it's not so warm now..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> the heat transfer from the metal tube to the intake air may be minimal but any intake air heat is bad heat.   <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstair1.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstair1.jpg</A> <P>cwestco makes good stuff... you just have to have a better performing car to realize thier products full potential. <P>i was upset that my headers killed my low end performance.. but the MONSTEROUS torque gain under the spray was nice. from 253lbft to 310lbft. but for daily driving.. i was kinda bummed.  but i got over it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> now.. whats next... i think i'll try the shortys. ||||reg||1
Z||000026||Steven||04-02-2001||04:17 PM||||Mr. West, have you ever explored the idea of making a tube that went from the oem air cleaner to the throttle body for those of use who might be unsure of the cold air intake? I mean a tube that was smooth on the inside I would think could flow air better than the factory rubber piece with all of its bumps and ridges that came stock on V-6 Fiero. ||||reg||5
Z||000027||Black88GT||04-02-2001||04:56 PM||||Check the website I think its called the "velocity tube"||||reg||1
Z||000028||saleenfiero||04-02-2001||05:31 PM||||what is a k&N extreme filter?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000029||c m west||04-02-2001||06:05 PM||||The K&N Extreme Filtercharger is a conical element filter. Essentially shapped like a cone with an inlet area at the the tip like import filters, except with the waranty and satisfaction of K&N, <P> we havent messed around with making a tube from the canister to the throttle body because there would be absolutly no gain, maybe gain for cosmetics only, we try not to sell gimmicks here.<P> As far as lieing to the computer about false MAT sensor temps this would cause poor running do to the computer compensating by having the cold start valve inject more fuel into the motor. This would relate to poor fuel economy, poor drivabilty, and no variable curve for the computer in open loop.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Steven||04-02-2001||06:08 PM||||No, the Velocity Tube replaces the air box which is below the air cleaner. What I was talking about would be a tube that went from side of air cleaner to the throttle body. ||||reg||1
Z||000031||c m west||04-02-2001||06:12 PM||||Good or bad - its still good to see responses in general, hehe - lets keep this going......<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Black88GT||04-02-2001||06:25 PM||||Eric, is that availible in red?||||reg||1
Z||000033||c m west||04-02-2001||06:47 PM||||let me know what color you want, and Ill paint it, we use durable spray paint on these tubes being that they are steel and we do not wish for any rust to form during shipment, not that it would, but its also nice for the "wow" factor when a customer puls it out of the box.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Sleeper||04-02-2001||08:29 PM||||Black88GT - i have pretty much the stock exhaust and at a idle you hear the intake over the exhaust but when I get on it the exhaust is louder than the intake. so if you removed your cat your exhaust should still be a little louder than the intake. and what do you mean about a cover shot/pic?<P>Fyerstarter - in your post you couldn't have said it any better.<P>i also agree with want WKDFIRO said about Standard's post. its my car i drove in stock enough to know what it was capable of and now with the intake i feel a diferance in the car, its quicker/faster. now i know a dyno is complete proof but when my friend wanted to dyno his chevelle he called called a few places and the average price was like $99 for one run on the dyno or $300 for a full day. so i say fu*k that, i have better things to spend my money on. <P>and 1FST2M6 thanks for the info i'll look into some thermo-rap.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Sleeper (edited 04-02-2001).]||||reg||1
